<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Bit-Yakin</h1><p class="st-meta">880 BCE – 640 BCE (approx.) · capital Dur-Yakin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The most powerful Chaldean tribe of southern Babylonia, home of Merodach-baladan, the long-time rival of the kings of Assyria.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bit-Yakin occupied the marshes and coastland at the head of the Gulf, the region once called the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sealand-dynasty">Sealand</a>. Its fortified centre was Dur-Yakin. Like the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-tribes">Chaldean tribes</a>, it appears in Assyrian annals in the 9th century BCE, when its chiefs paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its sheikhs grew rich from date palms, Gulf trade and control of the marsh routes, which also made their land a refuge from Assyrian armies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merodach-baladan-ii">Merodach-baladan II</a> (Marduk-apla-iddina) made himself king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> from 721 to 710 BCE and again in 703 BCE, allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/neo-elamite-kingdom">Neo-Elamite kingdom</a> and sent envoys as far as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Judah</a>, an embassy mentioned in the Bible.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> took Dur-Yakin in 709 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> pursued the tribe's refugees by ship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> in 694 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merodach-baladan-ii">Merodach-baladan</a>'s grandson Nabu-bel-shumati joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamash-shum-ukin">Shamash-shum-ukin's</a> revolt and died in Elam around 648 BCE, after which Bit-Yakin disappears as a power.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bit-yakin.jpg" alt="Bit-Yakin" width="320" height="242"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur; derivative work by Zunkir · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Babylonie_chaldeens.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>880 BCE – 640 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dur-Yakin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Merodach-baladan II, Nabu-bel-shumati</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Babylonian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic, Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bit Yakin</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-760&amp;focus=bit-yakin" aria-label="Bit-Yakin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5777 974 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bit-Yakin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0M6140 1251h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6097 1230h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6097 1230h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iraq. <a href="/atlas?year=-760&amp;focus=bit-yakin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
